                <pb facs="0189" n="93" sameAs="frus:frus1926v02/pg93"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">800.51 W 89France/313a: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Herrick</hi>)</head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1926-06-02T17:00:00-04:00"><hi rendition="#italic">June 2, 1926—5
                                p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>153. By a vote of 226 for and 111 against, the House of Representatives has
                    adopted the French debt settlement. The Senate will not act until action has
                    been taken by the French Parliament. The Congress is ready to adjourn and is
                    simply awaiting French action on adoption of the debt settlement. What prospects
                    are there for speedy action?</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Kellogg</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
